WEBVTT

00:03.672 --> 00:06.114
-(METAL DOOR CREAKING)
-(CLOCK TICKING)

00:11.753 --> 00:13.517
(CREAKING CONTINUES)

00:15.758 --> 00:17.422
(FOOTSTEPS APPROACHING)

00:19.024 --> 00:21.558
(METAL DOOR OPENING)

00:24.327 --> 00:25.760
(METAL DOOR SLAMS SHUT)

00:28.368 --> 00:31.370
-(TICKING CONTINUES)
-(FOOTSTEPS APPROACHING)

00:32.840 --> 00:34.576
(METAL DOOR OPENS)

00:34.678 --> 00:35.605
PRISON GUARD: Open 15.

00:37.409 --> 00:39.411
-(DOOR LOCK CLICKING)
-(DOOR BUZZING)

00:40.344 --> 00:42.950
(METAL DOOR RUMBLING, OPENING)

00:43.052 --> 00:44.948
-(FOOTSTEPS APPROACHING)
-(KEYS JANGLING)

00:45.050 --> 00:46.720
(SHEETS RUSTLING)

00:46.822 --> 00:48.081
PRISON GUARD: Hey, get up!

00:48.517 --> 00:50.458
You got a visitor. Look alive.

00:50.560 --> 00:51.759
(DOOR BUZZING)

00:51.861 --> 00:54.360
(METAL DOOR RUMBLING, OPENING)

00:58.127 --> 00:59.765
Becket.

01:00.467 --> 01:03.569
I'm Father James Morris,
one of the chaplains here.

01:04.004 --> 01:05.503
How are you feeling today?

01:09.372 --> 01:10.640
(BECKET GRUNTS SOFTLY)

01:17.213 --> 01:18.450
Cheesecake?

01:19.116 --> 01:20.151
No, thank you.

01:20.253 --> 01:22.449
BECKET: You know,
I ordered vanilla

01:22.551 --> 01:24.126
and they brought me chocolate.

01:25.089 --> 01:26.326
Kill me now.

01:27.823 --> 01:30.762
So, Father, appreciate you
coming down here,

01:30.864 --> 01:33.593
but I'm fine.

01:33.695 --> 01:35.970
Well, forgive me,
but most people

01:36.072 --> 01:38.237
in your position
feel a bit differently.

01:38.340 --> 01:40.169
Well, I'd argue
most people in my position,

01:40.271 --> 01:41.405
most people, in general,

01:41.507 --> 01:44.278
lack my sense of clarity,
presence of mind.

01:45.345 --> 01:46.542
FATHER MORRIS:
I don't mean to overstep,

01:46.644 --> 01:48.783
but, um, this may be
the last opportunity

01:48.885 --> 01:51.615
you have to speak to anyone.

01:53.683 --> 01:55.586
Is there nothing
you'd like to share?

01:56.990 --> 01:59.161
(TICKING CONTINUES)

02:01.460 --> 02:04.166
BECKET: You know
why I'm here, right?

02:05.631 --> 02:08.170
-I know a little.
-BECKET: Well, it's not true.

02:10.468 --> 02:13.373
The real story,
it's much more...

02:15.271 --> 02:17.109
impressive.

02:17.211 --> 02:19.247
Do you want
to tell me about it?

02:21.177 --> 02:22.382
Well...

02:23.753 --> 02:24.846
I should warn you...

02:26.888 --> 02:27.948
it's a tragedy.

02:29.488 --> 02:31.457
And it doesn't even
start with me.

02:32.057 --> 02:33.560
Funny enough,

02:33.996 --> 02:35.461
it starts with someone else.

02:36.967 --> 02:38.164
 And by that I mean, my mother.

02:38.266 --> 02:39.966
...has completely
forgotten how to play.

02:40.068 --> 02:43.238
 ...Mary Estella Redfellow.

02:43.340 --> 02:45.801
 Longtime resident
 of Huntington, Long Island,

02:47.407 --> 02:49.605
 an heiress
 to a family fortune

02:49.708 --> 02:53.180
 valued then at 18,
 19 billion US dollars.

02:53.282 --> 02:55.149
-Thank you.
-(INDISTINCT CONVERSATIONS)

02:55.251 --> 02:56.347
BECKET: Now,
 there's a rumor out there

02:56.449 --> 02:58.481
 that money
 doesn't buy happiness.

02:58.583 --> 03:00.549
Dead wrong.
Money does buy happiness.

03:00.651 --> 03:02.418
We're all adults here.
Let's move on.

03:02.520 --> 03:04.290
(SOFT INSTRUMENTAL
MUSIC PLAYING)

03:04.393 --> 03:06.586
 I'm told that the night
 she met my father

03:06.688 --> 03:08.461
 was a night like any other.

03:11.532 --> 03:14.198
 And by that, I mean
 she was young, rich.

03:14.300 --> 03:15.930
 What could ever go wrong?

03:17.939 --> 03:19.307
(KNOCKING ON DOOR)

03:25.709 --> 03:28.144
-MARY: What time is it?
-(COW MOOING)

03:28.246 --> 03:29.180
MARY: Oh, my God!

03:29.283 --> 03:31.119
-CELLIST: What?
-Oh, my God,

03:31.221 --> 03:32.980
-I have to go right now.
-You gotta go now?

03:33.657 --> 03:34.685
MARY: Bye.

03:40.689 --> 03:42.424
(LAUGHING) What are you doing?

03:42.526 --> 03:44.398
-MARY: I have to go.
-Okay.

03:50.866 --> 03:53.275
MAN: I applaud you
 for telling us.

03:53.810 --> 03:55.439
 That being said,

03:55.541 --> 03:57.543
I think you know
what has to happen.

03:58.846 --> 03:59.779
I'm 18.

03:59.881 --> 04:01.041
Tragically.

04:02.244 --> 04:03.610
No.

04:03.712 --> 04:05.452
This should be my decision.

04:07.351 --> 04:08.620
This should be me.

04:11.893 --> 04:12.953
All right.

04:13.055 --> 04:15.825
You can either get rid of it
or do what you like.

04:17.393 --> 04:18.630
But not here.

04:19.902 --> 04:20.962
Not under this roof.

04:22.299 --> 04:24.032
(SIGHS SOFTLY)

04:24.134 --> 04:26.402
BECKET: In this way, she went
 from being Mary Redfellow

04:26.504 --> 04:28.072
 of Huntington, New York...

04:28.174 --> 04:29.844
(VEHICLES RUSHING)

04:29.946 --> 04:31.038
 ...to Mary Redfellow

04:31.140 --> 04:32.772
 -of Belleville, New Jersey.
 - (TRUCK HORN BLOWS)

04:32.874 --> 04:34.679
-(SCREAMING)
-(DOCTOR MURMURING)

04:35.444 --> 04:36.514
DOCTOR: Breathe.

04:36.617 --> 04:38.151
-(SCREAMING CONTINUES)
-(OBJECTS CLATTERING)

04:38.253 --> 04:39.216
DOCTOR: You're doing well.
Breathe.

04:39.318 --> 04:40.319
MARY: I can't. No, no.

04:40.421 --> 04:41.684
BECKET My father saw me
 only once...

04:41.787 --> 04:43.688
-CELLIST: Oh, my God!
-(OBJECTS CLATTERING)

04:43.790 --> 04:45.854
 ...which was enough to give
 him a pulmonary embolism

04:45.956 --> 04:48.460
 right there at Jersey City
 Memorial Hospital.

04:48.562 --> 04:50.230
...a vehicle that we haven't
seen before...

04:50.332 --> 04:52.599
BECKET: My mother
 was left alone

04:52.701 --> 04:55.336
 to work at the Newark
 Department of Motor Vehicles.

04:55.438 --> 04:56.997
-(TELEPHONE RINGING)
-(INDISTINCT CONVERSATIONS)

04:57.099 --> 04:59.737
 Pretty soon, she told me
 something important.

05:00.603 --> 05:01.904
MARY: In the painting...

05:02.006 --> 05:03.344
BECKET: We may have been
 kicked out of the family,

05:03.446 --> 05:05.677
 but we weren't kicked
 out of the inheritance.

05:07.309 --> 05:10.677
 See, my grandfather had
 drawn up an irrevocable trust

05:10.780 --> 05:12.316
 to avoid paying estate taxes.

05:12.418 --> 05:14.352
 It meant that the fortune,

05:14.454 --> 05:16.484
 the whole thing,
 the real estate,

05:16.586 --> 05:18.424
 the boats, the planes,

05:18.526 --> 05:20.625
 an island or two,
 would be passed down

05:20.727 --> 05:23.563
 to the next oldest in line,
 no matter what.

05:24.430 --> 05:25.860
 And me, being the youngest,

05:25.963 --> 05:28.431
 I might inherit
 the entire thing someday,

05:28.534 --> 05:30.301
 assuming I outlive the others.

05:30.403 --> 05:32.270
(MARY SPEAKING INDISTINCTLY)

05:32.372 --> 05:33.672
BECKET: I just had to wait.

05:36.507 --> 05:37.775
For all of them to die.

05:38.809 --> 05:41.278
-(TICKING CONTINUES)
-(DOOR BUZZING)

05:41.380 --> 05:43.484
(UPBEAT MUSIC PLAYING)

05:47.018 --> 05:49.450
BECKET: Now a single mother
 doing it all herself,

05:49.552 --> 05:50.621
 you might assume

05:50.723 --> 05:52.592
 -life was tough, but...
 - (DOG BARKING)

05:53.260 --> 05:54.395
 ...I'll tell you now...

05:54.497 --> 05:55.757
MARY: 'Cause they all sound
the same once you...

05:55.859 --> 05:58.929
 ...despite our
 financial handicap,

05:59.031 --> 06:01.936
 she made sure I had all
 the culture, class, and style

06:02.038 --> 06:03.436
 of a highborn young man.

06:03.538 --> 06:05.539
So when it goes,
it goes through the target.

06:06.871 --> 06:08.604
See how much
of a difference that makes?

06:08.706 --> 06:10.243
-YOUNG BECKET: Mmm-hmm.
-See?

06:10.345 --> 06:11.539
You just open up
your shoulders.

06:11.641 --> 06:13.547
That is so much better.

06:14.784 --> 06:15.513
Okay.

06:15.615 --> 06:17.111
BECKET: That was
 the first time...

06:18.253 --> 06:19.949
 the first time I saw her.

06:23.652 --> 06:24.921
Julia Steinway.

06:27.960 --> 06:31.092
 Right off, I could see
 we were the perfect match.

06:31.194 --> 06:33.265
-(CHILDREN EXCLAIMING)
- And clearly, I wasn't wrong.

06:33.367 --> 06:34.567
 This was, without a doubt,

06:34.669 --> 06:38.966
 a girl of great
 social bearing, taste, charm.

06:39.068 --> 06:40.838
JULIA'S MOTHER: What's next?
The big one?

06:41.636 --> 06:42.936
(HORSE WHINNIES)

06:43.038 --> 06:44.440
BECKET: I was
 in the right place.

06:44.542 --> 06:46.613
(INDISTINCT CONVERSATIONS)

06:50.449 --> 06:51.816
What are you doing here?

06:54.023 --> 06:55.116
Your mom invited me.

06:55.992 --> 06:57.452
Where do you live?

06:57.554 --> 06:58.790
Belleville Township.

06:59.788 --> 07:01.190
Do you live on the street?

07:01.292 --> 07:02.563
What?

07:02.665 --> 07:04.664
YOUNG JULIA: I heard people
there live on the street.

07:05.432 --> 07:06.897
I don't live on the street.

07:07.799 --> 07:09.434
And I mean, I won't
be there forever

07:09.536 --> 07:11.503
because I'm supposed
to inherit

07:11.605 --> 07:13.207
a lot of money
or something, so...

07:13.309 --> 07:14.608
YOUNG JULIA:
A lot of money, huh?

07:15.640 --> 07:17.171
Are you trying to impress me?

07:19.643 --> 07:20.911
It's okay if you are.

07:22.349 --> 07:24.079
(SOFT PIANO MUSIC PLAYING)

07:36.099 --> 07:37.565
(AUDIENCE APPLAUDING)

07:40.470 --> 07:42.504
PIANO TEACHER:
Next, we have Lyle Archdale!

07:46.408 --> 07:48.070
(HIGH-TEMPO
PIANO MUSIC PLAYING)

07:53.248 --> 07:54.747
(DISTANT THUNDER RUMBLING)

07:55.348 --> 07:57.451
(ELECTRICAL BUZZING)

07:57.553 --> 07:59.147
-(PIANO MUSIC STOPS)
-(AUDIENCE MURMURING)

08:00.657 --> 08:02.755
PIANO TEACHER: It's all right.
Everyone, it's okay.

08:04.258 --> 08:05.355
Lyle, can you see?

08:05.457 --> 08:06.490
Just keep playing.

08:06.592 --> 08:07.556
(YOUNG BECKET INHALES SHARPLY)

08:07.658 --> 08:08.827
(PIANO MUSIC RESUMES)

08:16.369 --> 08:17.539
(ELECTRICAL BUZZING)

08:30.812 --> 08:31.982
(PIANO MUSIC FADES)

08:35.322 --> 08:36.591
DOCTOR: Hey, Becket.

08:37.292 --> 08:38.560
Can you come with me?

08:42.498 --> 08:44.360
BECKET:
 Well, my mother was young...

08:44.996 --> 08:46.596
 which is why
 she hadn't paid attention

08:46.698 --> 08:48.636
 to the symptoms at first.

08:49.603 --> 08:52.167
-Doctors either.
-(CLOCK TICKING)

08:53.169 --> 08:56.270
And the family, um,
I'm sure they knew.

08:57.742 --> 08:59.812
I'm sure they could've helped.

09:01.248 --> 09:02.210
But nothing.

09:04.681 --> 09:06.915
Not one single word.

09:07.017 --> 09:09.118
MARY: Becket,
 this is all wrong.

09:12.659 --> 09:13.892
YOUNG BECKET:
What's all wrong?

09:16.432 --> 09:18.193
You should have
more than this.

09:21.029 --> 09:23.297
Something was taken from you.

09:27.169 --> 09:29.743
And I want you
to promise me something.

09:32.313 --> 09:33.681
Will you do that?

09:35.017 --> 09:36.347
Okay.

09:36.449 --> 09:37.685
MARY: Promise me...

09:38.881 --> 09:40.787
that you won't quit...

09:42.085 --> 09:45.253
until you have
the right kind of life.

09:48.057 --> 09:49.559
Until you have
the kind of life

09:49.661 --> 09:52.799
that you deserve to have.

09:53.863 --> 09:55.670
That I raised you to have.

09:57.900 --> 09:59.641
Can you do that for me?

10:04.976 --> 10:07.209
BECKET:
 "The right kind of life."

10:08.681 --> 10:10.245
 What did she mean by that?

10:11.782 --> 10:13.215
(CHORAL MUSIC PLAYING)

10:15.091 --> 10:16.053
(QUACKING)

10:18.019 --> 10:19.087
BECKET: Her final request

10:19.190 --> 10:21.726
 was to be buried
 at the Redfellow Mausoleum

10:21.828 --> 10:23.896
 on the family plot
 in Huntington.

10:24.764 --> 10:25.695
PRIEST: We are
 gathered here...

10:25.797 --> 10:27.062
BECKET: When no answer came,

10:27.164 --> 10:29.767
 she arrived
 at her final resting place

10:29.869 --> 10:32.806
 just off the parkway
 in East Orange.

10:34.171 --> 10:36.870
PRIEST: And Jesus tells us,
"I am the way,

10:36.972 --> 10:38.776
"the truth, and the life.

10:38.878 --> 10:41.979
"He who has faith..."
Or she indeed...

10:42.081 --> 10:44.185
BECKET: In that moment,
 I made up my mind.

10:44.287 --> 10:45.346
 Right then and there.

10:48.756 --> 10:51.220
 I would go straight
 to the source

10:51.322 --> 10:55.059
 and demand my rightful place
 at my rightful home.

10:57.167 --> 10:58.898
(DOORBELL BUZZES)

11:15.750 --> 11:18.218
-(MUSIC CONTINUES)
-(CAR DOOR CLOSES)

11:18.320 --> 11:19.281
(ENGINE STARTS)

11:22.191 --> 11:23.450
(CAR DEPARTS)

11:27.023 --> 11:29.225
"We hope this finds you well."

11:32.362 --> 11:37.934
After that, I enjoyed
a brief and colorful tour

11:38.036 --> 11:40.170
of the New Jersey
foster care system.

11:41.910 --> 11:45.241
 As for Julia,
 I saw her less and less...

11:47.217 --> 11:48.211
 until finally...

11:50.385 --> 11:51.445
 never at all.

11:53.417 --> 11:56.824
 And I had to wonder
 if we crossed paths again,

11:57.953 --> 11:59.222
 who would I be?

12:00.230 --> 12:01.224
 A great success?

12:02.090 --> 12:04.293
-(MUSIC STOPS)
- Or no?

12:05.393 --> 12:09.299
-(HORNS HONKING)
-(INDISTINCT CHATTER)

12:09.401 --> 12:12.604
BECKET: Anyway, many years
 later, I found myself working

12:12.706 --> 12:14.909
 a perfectly respectable job,

12:15.011 --> 12:18.006
 the kind where you wear a suit
 to the office every day.

12:18.108 --> 12:20.046
-Morning.
-DARRYL: You're early.

12:20.281 --> 12:21.443
Yes, sir.

12:21.545 --> 12:24.646
Uh, why don't you jump on that
order from uptown, please?

12:24.748 --> 12:25.680
Upper West.

12:25.782 --> 12:27.151
(DOOR CLOSING)

12:29.886 --> 12:31.056
(KEYS JANGLING)

12:32.955 --> 12:34.355
(KEYBOARD CLACKING)

12:34.457 --> 12:36.428
BECKET: I still kept track
 of the Redfellows

12:36.530 --> 12:38.195
 and the estate.

12:38.297 --> 12:42.364
 Sadly, their mortality rate
 was irritatingly low.

12:43.539 --> 12:45.101
 And I'd pretty much accepted

12:45.204 --> 12:48.002
 that I'd never see
 a penny of the fortune.

12:48.105 --> 12:50.141
 Not in this lifetime, anyway.

12:51.447 --> 12:52.880
 And maybe that was fine.

13:03.819 --> 13:05.255
Becket?

13:06.663 --> 13:09.061
-No fucking way! (LAUGHS)
-(EXHALES)

13:10.599 --> 13:12.333
-Wow.
-BECKET: Oh, my God.

13:12.435 --> 13:14.194
Oh, my God. Hi! (LAUGHS)

13:14.296 --> 13:16.563
Yeah. Wow.
(LAUGHING) Oh, my...

13:17.737 --> 13:18.936
I can't believe it's you.

13:19.038 --> 13:21.542
I can't believe
it's me either. How...

13:21.644 --> 13:23.911
-Wow.
-What's with the, uh...

13:25.010 --> 13:26.574
Are you in
the Secret Service or...

13:26.676 --> 13:28.008
Oh.

13:28.110 --> 13:30.276
-Yes.
-JULIA: Yeah.

13:30.378 --> 13:32.553
BECKET: Can't really talk
about it.

13:32.655 --> 13:33.683
So, are you living
in the city now?

13:33.785 --> 13:35.321
Yeah, I do. I do.

13:35.423 --> 13:36.723
A little...

13:36.825 --> 13:38.051
-A little outside of it.
-JULIA: Oh.

13:38.153 --> 13:40.854
Um, are you still in...

13:40.956 --> 13:42.362
What was it?

13:42.464 --> 13:44.431
Belleville Township.

13:44.533 --> 13:45.464
-Right.
-BECKET: So...

13:45.566 --> 13:46.560
You're still there?

13:48.504 --> 13:50.000
How about you? Are you...

13:50.102 --> 13:51.301
What, are you still
in Montclair?

13:51.403 --> 13:56.636
Yeah. I was in London
for a while and then Dubai,

13:56.738 --> 14:00.508
but now I'm back in
the old country. (SMACKS LIPS)

14:01.548 --> 14:02.576
Wow. Well...

14:03.748 --> 14:05.317
I mean, I'd...

14:05.887 --> 14:08.351
I'd love to properly
catch up if...

14:09.659 --> 14:11.387
you want to grab
a drink sometime?

14:12.823 --> 14:14.220
That'd be great.

14:14.323 --> 14:15.930
But the timing's
sort of, um...

14:16.893 --> 14:18.130
Just the timing.

14:19.829 --> 14:21.067
Sure.

14:21.633 --> 14:22.603
Sure.

14:22.705 --> 14:23.663
JULIA: It was great
to see you.

14:25.103 --> 14:26.633
I'm glad
you're still like this.

14:27.740 --> 14:29.669
Like... Like what?

14:30.879 --> 14:34.047
(SMACKS LIPS)
Just like yourself.

14:36.252 --> 14:38.480
Oh, um...

14:38.582 --> 14:41.483
Whatever happened to
the Redfellow family fortune?

14:42.919 --> 14:44.123
I'll inherit it.

14:45.228 --> 14:46.992
Someday.

14:47.094 --> 14:49.491
Well, call me
when you've killed them all.

14:51.093 --> 14:53.294
(DOOR OPENS, CLOSES)

14:53.396 --> 14:56.100
JULIA: Lyle,
Becket's inside there.

14:56.203 --> 14:57.700
LYLE: Becket?
JULIA: Do you remember Becket

14:57.802 --> 14:59.465
from school?

14:59.567 --> 15:01.437
LYLE: (SCOFFS)
Of course he works here.

15:03.439 --> 15:06.211
-(THUNDER RUMBLING)
-(RAIN PATTERING)

15:17.824 --> 15:19.323
(GENTLE MUSIC PLAYING)

15:31.336 --> 15:34.239
MARY: Promise me
 that you won't quit...

15:35.307 --> 15:38.111
 until you have
 the right kind of life.

15:38.975 --> 15:40.476
(MUSIC FADES)

15:46.152 --> 15:47.483
BECKET: There were
 seven of them.

15:48.418 --> 15:51.386
 Seven Redfellows
 walking the earth

15:51.488 --> 15:53.626
 while my mother
 lay cold in the ground

15:53.728 --> 15:55.293
 and I lived in a duplex.

15:58.494 --> 16:00.967
 If I were to prune
 a few branches

16:01.070 --> 16:04.101
 of the family tree,
 where would I start?

16:04.203 --> 16:05.539
(HIP-HOP MUSIC
PLAYING OVER SPEAKERS)

16:05.641 --> 16:06.700
(INDISTINCT CONVERSATIONS)

16:21.553 --> 16:25.588
 -Taylor Exeter Redfellow.
-Thank you very much.

16:25.690 --> 16:28.559
BECKET: Broker at his father's
 financial service firm.

16:28.928 --> 16:29.996
(INDISTINCT CONVERSATION)

16:30.099 --> 16:32.593
BECKET: My cousin wasn't
 just easy to find,

16:32.695 --> 16:34.327
 he was hard to avoid
 once you got anywhere

16:34.429 --> 16:36.003
 near the Financial District.

16:36.105 --> 16:38.267
♪ Wish somebody would tell me
 I'm fine ♪

16:38.369 --> 16:41.138
♪ Losing my sight
 Losing my mind ♪

16:41.240 --> 16:43.439
♪ Wish somebody
 would tell... ♪

16:43.541 --> 16:46.608
BECKET: Even if I did it,
 how would I do it?

16:48.243 --> 16:49.243
 And what then?

16:49.345 --> 16:50.913
 Would I collapse in regret?

16:51.015 --> 16:53.184
 Turn myself in?

16:53.287 --> 16:55.618
(SCOFFS) Let's face it,
 who am I kidding?

16:55.720 --> 16:57.892
 I couldn't possibly be capable
 of something like that.

16:57.994 --> 17:00.659
DARRYL: Yo, let me
grab you for a minute.

17:00.761 --> 17:01.728
BECKET: I don't...

17:01.830 --> 17:03.061
I don't think I understand.

17:03.163 --> 17:04.897
We just think
you might be happier.

17:04.999 --> 17:06.964
If I transferred
to Jersey City?

17:07.066 --> 17:08.262
It's closer to where you live.

17:08.365 --> 17:09.829
-They have parking.
-Parking.

17:12.404 --> 17:14.106
Are you talking about
the shipping center?

17:14.208 --> 17:16.440
All right. Vic's kid
dropped out of school.

17:16.542 --> 17:19.209
You're giving my job to
the owner's 19-year-old kid?

17:19.311 --> 17:20.745
DARRYL: I'm not doing
anything, man.

17:20.847 --> 17:22.215
This wasn't my idea.

17:23.678 --> 17:25.612
I don't want to work
in a warehouse.

17:25.714 --> 17:27.787
DARRYL: It's inconvenient
for me, too, okay?

17:27.890 --> 17:29.988
-Help me out.
-BECKET: Darryl.

17:30.090 --> 17:31.188
-DARRYL: Please?
-I'm good at this.

17:31.290 --> 17:34.489
-I work hard, I show up.
-I know, I know.

17:34.591 --> 17:36.724
Shouldn't that sort of thing
be rewarded?

17:39.327 --> 17:40.464
(KNUCKLES CRACKING)

17:45.971 --> 17:47.174
(INDISTINCT CHATTER)

17:49.141 --> 17:51.145
(UPBEAT PARTY MUSIC PLAYING)

17:57.149 --> 17:59.351
(HELICOPTER BLADES WHIRRING)

18:01.250 --> 18:03.124
(MUSIC CONTINUES)

18:05.156 --> 18:06.853
(INDISTINCT CONVERSATIONS)

18:13.562 --> 18:15.800
MAN: Bro,
this is ridiculous!

18:15.902 --> 18:16.998
TAYLOR: Holy shit!

18:17.100 --> 18:19.239
(ALL CHEERING)

18:25.409 --> 18:26.840
-(BLOWS RASPBERRY)
-Come on, man.

18:26.942 --> 18:28.210
-Are you gonna fucking do it?
-I'm fucking ready.

18:28.313 --> 18:30.046
-Come on!
-I'm fucking ready!

18:30.148 --> 18:31.280
-MAN: Go!
-Let's go!

18:31.382 --> 18:32.681
-I'm doing it!
-Let's fucking go!

18:34.986 --> 18:36.850
(CROWD CHEERING)

18:42.224 --> 18:43.395
TAYLOR: Yeah!

18:46.130 --> 18:47.663
(CHEERING CONTINUES)

18:50.135 --> 18:51.101
TAYLOR: Yeah! Pick it up.

18:51.203 --> 18:53.900
You're still poor, assholes!

18:55.140 --> 18:57.039
-Whoa!
-TAYLOR: Whoo!

18:57.141 --> 18:58.311
(TAYLOR SCREAMING)

19:01.682 --> 19:03.580
Yeah!

19:05.049 --> 19:07.188
Eat the money!

19:10.719 --> 19:11.885
-Hey!
-(INDISTINCT CONVERSATIONS)

19:17.499 --> 19:20.762
BECKET: Just like before,
 my cousin was easy to find.

19:20.864 --> 19:24.301
 I didn't have a plan exactly,
 but the proverbial

19:24.403 --> 19:26.566
 and literal doors
 were wide open.

19:26.668 --> 19:28.202
-(FIRE BLAZING)
-(ALL CHEERING)

19:28.304 --> 19:29.969
(MUSIC CONTINUES)

19:37.884 --> 19:39.814
(WHOOPING)

19:40.953 --> 19:42.583
(MUSIC FADES)

19:42.685 --> 19:45.092
TAYLOR: World Star,
motherfucker!

19:45.194 --> 19:47.156
BECKET: Could this be
 my opportunity

19:47.258 --> 19:48.856
 to get Taylor alone?

19:50.227 --> 19:51.557
 At this point,

19:51.659 --> 19:53.399
I could have gone home and...

19:54.435 --> 19:56.930
maybe I would have
on a different day, but...

19:58.136 --> 19:59.566
as the Bible says,

19:59.668 --> 20:01.237
"The journey
of a thousand miles

20:01.339 --> 20:03.508
"begins with a single step."

20:06.147 --> 20:07.579
-Does the Bible say that?
-No.

20:07.681 --> 20:08.645
Oh.

20:12.248 --> 20:13.683
(ENGINE ROARING)

20:17.021 --> 20:17.984
(COUGHS)

20:18.893 --> 20:21.460
(GROANS, SIGHS)

20:22.225 --> 20:23.429
Oh, thank God.

20:23.531 --> 20:26.432
They put fucking low ethanol
in the engine. (CHUCKLES)

20:27.133 --> 20:29.864
(SNIFFS) Get the anchor.

20:31.770 --> 20:35.906
Call fucking Baywatch.
Call David-fucking-Hasselhoff.

20:36.008 --> 20:37.242
It's my boat.

20:37.344 --> 20:39.313
-(FAINT RADIO CHATTER)
-(TAYLOR GROANS)

20:40.376 --> 20:41.777
TAYLOR: Ah, fuck.

20:44.381 --> 20:47.151
My helicopter. (CHUCKLES)

20:47.253 --> 20:50.324
(COUGHS) That's my jet ski.

20:55.059 --> 20:56.429
(SNORES)

20:58.694 --> 21:00.565
-(ANCHOR THUDS)
-(TAYLOR COUGHS)

21:03.971 --> 21:05.306
(CARABINER CLICKS)

21:23.924 --> 21:25.084
(TAYLOR EXHALES)

21:28.324 --> 21:30.089
(CHAIN RATTLING)

21:38.005 --> 21:39.432
BECKET: Time out.

21:40.068 --> 21:42.736
 I gotta take a minute,
 think this through.

21:42.838 --> 21:44.144
 Process what
 I'm actually doing here,

21:44.246 --> 21:45.177
 and if I'm carrying
 this out...

21:45.279 --> 21:47.678
-(TAYLOR COUGHS)
-(ANCHOR THUDS)

21:48.376 --> 21:49.515
Wait.

21:51.215 --> 21:52.711
Who are you?

21:52.814 --> 21:54.648
-I'm Becket.
-(ROPE WHOOSHING)

21:54.750 --> 21:57.358
-TAYLOR: Whoa!
-(WATER SPLASHING)

21:58.653 --> 22:00.460
(RADIO CHATTER CONTINUES)

22:03.964 --> 22:05.630
(WATER RIPPLING)

22:08.002 --> 22:09.029
BECKET: By God.

22:10.102 --> 22:11.130
 Was that it?

22:11.940 --> 22:12.967
 Was it that easy?

22:13.468 --> 22:14.639
(ENGINE ROARING)

22:15.838 --> 22:18.445
 I hate to say yes,
 but it is true.

22:18.547 --> 22:22.442
 And if the whole thing
 gave you a strange thrill...

22:23.144 --> 22:25.386
Well, I guess you and I
have something in common.

22:29.417 --> 22:30.589
Anyway...

22:31.986 --> 22:33.722
can you believe I
attended the funeral?

22:33.824 --> 22:35.561
-(BELL TOLLING)
-(QUIRKY MUSIC PLAYING)

22:42.333 --> 22:43.668
(PEOPLE MURMURING)

22:44.532 --> 22:45.901
(PILLS RATTLING)

22:53.981 --> 22:55.174
(DUCKS QUACKING)

22:57.083 --> 22:58.144
Excuse me.

22:59.285 --> 23:00.685
Are you Warren Redfellow?

23:01.385 --> 23:02.588
Yeah, that's right.

23:04.293 --> 23:05.989
I'm... I'm so sorry
for your loss.

23:06.091 --> 23:07.120
Thanks.

23:08.030 --> 23:09.524
I'm not sure I, uh...

23:09.627 --> 23:11.124
I'm not sure
I know your name.

23:13.033 --> 23:14.094
I'm Becket.

23:16.171 --> 23:18.670
Mary's son. I'm your nephew.

23:21.043 --> 23:22.838
You're fucking with me.

23:22.940 --> 23:25.946
Eighteen years old,
she had the guts to stand up

23:26.048 --> 23:26.940
to the son of a bitch.

23:27.043 --> 23:29.846
None of us could've done it.
Not me, at least.

23:31.451 --> 23:33.848
I should've kept
in better touch.

23:33.950 --> 23:36.149
Been part of her life,
part of your life.

23:37.592 --> 23:39.125
I wish I would've gotten
a chance to know Taylor.

23:39.228 --> 23:40.793
-He seemed like a...
-(GRUNTS)

23:40.896 --> 23:42.260
I'm sorry.

23:42.363 --> 23:43.359
-Don't apologize.
-It's... No.

23:43.461 --> 23:45.763
Don't apologize. Really.

23:47.400 --> 23:48.733
You know who did it to him?

23:52.136 --> 23:53.067
Huh?

23:59.513 --> 24:00.673
I assure you,
that's not the case.

24:00.775 --> 24:02.043
WARREN: I wasn't there.

24:03.778 --> 24:05.684
When he was coming up...

24:05.786 --> 24:08.684
And I knew damn well that
he was going off the handle

24:08.786 --> 24:11.184
with the cars
and the prescription.

24:13.191 --> 24:14.187
(SLURPS)

24:15.358 --> 24:16.596
Anyway...

24:18.133 --> 24:19.258
what do you do, Becket?

24:20.662 --> 24:22.132
(LIGHTS POWERING ON)

24:22.234 --> 24:23.633
BECKET: Oh!

24:23.735 --> 24:25.171
WARREN: Yeah,
it's been in the family

24:25.274 --> 24:26.804
since La Guardia
was in office.

24:26.906 --> 24:31.308
I think every financial crisis
for the last 100 years

24:31.410 --> 24:32.777
started in the boardroom.

24:34.347 --> 24:36.110
I was here for two of 'em.

24:37.981 --> 24:40.114
All right,
welcome to the inner sanctum.

24:41.623 --> 24:44.050
-There, take a seat.
-BECKET: Well, this is nice.

24:44.152 --> 24:46.325
WARREN: I got this bottle
from Dick Cheney.

24:46.427 --> 24:48.056
-Better be good.
-(BOTTLE CLINKING)

24:49.730 --> 24:53.765
You know, we got Taylor's spot
downstairs and...

24:54.864 --> 24:58.030
Keep your head down,
ears open.

24:58.132 --> 25:01.166
Yeah. I'm not even
remotely qualified.

25:01.269 --> 25:03.476
-No shit. It's nepotism.
-BECKET: I just don't...

25:03.578 --> 25:06.976
He wasn't even
remotely qualified, either.

25:07.975 --> 25:09.412
Neither was I at one point.

25:09.514 --> 25:10.815
I guess the question is...

25:12.153 --> 25:13.112
would it be right?

25:13.214 --> 25:15.617
Becket, truth is,
I should've helped you out

25:15.719 --> 25:17.558
a long time ago, and I didn't.

25:19.589 --> 25:20.825
So let me do it now.

25:29.470 --> 25:31.198
BECKET: This was
 never the plan.

25:34.308 --> 25:36.076
 But I did need a job.

25:36.178 --> 25:37.104
(UPBEAT MUSIC PLAYING)

25:37.206 --> 25:38.711
Other than that, you just
gotta save your receipts,

25:38.813 --> 25:40.908
all right? If you don't do it,
then I can't help you.

25:41.010 --> 25:43.713
This is your home
for the next while.

25:43.815 --> 25:44.946
BECKET: Okay.
EMPLOYEE: Get going.

25:45.048 --> 25:46.417
-(TELEPHONE RINGING)
-EMPLOYEE 2: Hey, man,

25:46.519 --> 25:47.615
you already got this on email,

25:47.717 --> 25:48.714
but here's the hard copy.

25:48.816 --> 25:50.387
It goes up to 543,

25:50.489 --> 25:51.623
so you should be able

25:51.725 --> 25:52.756
-to hit 700 by close, right?
-BECKET: What?

25:52.858 --> 25:54.157
You can't let that ring.
You gotta answer it.

25:54.260 --> 25:55.695
Answer it.

25:58.094 --> 26:00.127
-Hello? How do you...
-(TELEPHONE RINGING)

26:00.229 --> 26:01.696
-How do you answer it?
-EMPLOYEE 1: The blue one.

26:01.799 --> 26:02.865
-The blue?
-EMPLOYEE 1: Yeah.

26:02.967 --> 26:04.231
-There's no blue.
-No, it didn't devalue.

26:04.333 --> 26:06.106
BECKET: Now, I won't bore you
 with the details.

26:06.208 --> 26:07.099
 But I will say this,

26:07.201 --> 26:08.608
 the wealthiest people
 in New York

26:08.710 --> 26:10.402
 don't actually make
 any money whatsoever.

26:10.504 --> 26:12.843
 They keep a percentage
 of what other people make,

26:12.945 --> 26:15.077
 which is a far more
 adaptable way of living

26:15.179 --> 26:17.517
 and one better suited
 to my overall disposition.

26:17.619 --> 26:18.882
That's all liquid.
I'm talking about debt.

26:18.984 --> 26:22.287
 In the first week alone,
 I learned the language.

26:22.389 --> 26:23.622
 Collateral, dividend,

26:23.724 --> 26:24.921
 -deduction, kicker...
-Okay, but I...

26:25.023 --> 26:28.461
 ...bailing, bootstrapping,
 closure, clawback,

26:28.563 --> 26:31.363
 lying, cheating,
 tailing, trickery.

26:31.465 --> 26:32.826
 I finally had a real job,

26:32.929 --> 26:34.662
 and I could use the education.

26:34.764 --> 26:35.833
(CELL PHONE VIBRATING)

26:35.935 --> 26:37.401
 I might have a fortune
 of my own to manage

26:37.503 --> 26:38.804
 someday soon enough.

26:39.505 --> 26:41.202
 Someday soon enough.

26:42.945 --> 26:43.907
Hello?

26:44.009 --> 26:45.541
JULIA: (OVER PHONE)
 Is this Becket Redfellow,

26:45.643 --> 26:47.307
 the new hire at W&R?

26:49.515 --> 26:51.649
Apologies, who's asking?

26:51.751 --> 26:53.313
JULIA: I hope
 this isn't a bad time.

26:55.188 --> 26:56.684
No. No.

26:56.786 --> 26:58.984
 Uh, how is married life?

26:59.086 --> 27:00.020
(FOOTBALL GAME CHATTER ON TV)

27:00.122 --> 27:02.459
We just got back
from our honeymoon.

27:02.561 --> 27:05.798
Wow. Greece? Maldives?

27:06.694 --> 27:07.866
Florida.

27:08.629 --> 27:09.934
Oh.

27:13.742 --> 27:16.171
Remember that time
you asked me out last year?

27:17.439 --> 27:21.176
You were folding khaki pants,
looking sad and pathetic?

27:23.449 --> 27:25.051
Doesn't ring a bell.

27:25.153 --> 27:26.181
I didn't mind.

27:27.451 --> 27:28.689
You know?

27:29.854 --> 27:31.351
 In case you felt embarrassed.

27:32.759 --> 27:34.695
 You could even
 try again sometime.

27:36.165 --> 27:37.592
See what happens.

27:37.694 --> 27:40.096
Try... Try again?

27:41.699 --> 27:43.231
JULIA: See what happens.

27:49.772 --> 27:50.975
Do you wanna...

27:52.173 --> 27:53.909
Do you wanna grab
a drink sometime?

27:54.011 --> 27:56.749
Well, no. (CLEARS THROAT)
I'm married.

27:56.851 --> 27:59.213
No, I... No, I... I know.
I mean, I just didn't...

27:59.315 --> 28:00.948
But I hope we run into
each other sometime soon.

28:01.050 --> 28:02.486
I always get
so much out of it.

28:02.588 --> 28:03.722
LYLE: Let's go! Come on!

28:03.824 --> 28:06.225
-(FOOTBALL GAME CONTINUES)
-LYLE: Perfect!

28:06.327 --> 28:07.827
(LINE DISCONNECTS)

28:10.993 --> 28:13.795
BECKET: Anyway, who's next?

28:13.898 --> 28:14.796
-(DRUMSTICKS CLACKING)
-Ooh!

28:14.899 --> 28:16.764
-(DRUMS POUNDING)
-Be like me!

28:16.866 --> 28:18.098
(HIP-HOP MUSIC PLAYING)

28:18.200 --> 28:20.536
Hello, Internet.
I'm Noah Redfellow.

28:20.638 --> 28:22.606
This is my domicile.

28:22.708 --> 28:24.204
(MIMICKING GUNSHOTS, GRUNTS)

28:24.306 --> 28:25.975
INTERVIEWER: And, like,
what would you say

28:26.078 --> 28:29.142
are the main struggles
as an artist these days?

28:29.244 --> 28:31.849
Probably, like,
imposter syndrome.

28:31.951 --> 28:34.148
-INTERVIEWER: Okay.
-Not me for myself,

28:34.250 --> 28:35.720
 but, like, from other
 people about me,

28:35.822 --> 28:37.021
 where they're like,
 "He's not...

28:37.123 --> 28:38.083
 "He's like... you're full
 of shit," or whatever.

28:38.185 --> 28:39.559
 And it's like, "No, dude."

28:39.661 --> 28:41.024
 That's fuckin'
 imposter syndrome.

28:41.126 --> 28:43.456
- Do you have a day job?
-NOAH: A day job? Yeah.

28:44.792 --> 28:46.030
Artist.

28:46.597 --> 28:47.663
(MUSIC FADES)

28:47.765 --> 28:49.429
(TECHNO MUSIC PLAYING)

28:55.605 --> 28:58.610
BECKET: Now, I can't say I was
 a professional at this point,

28:58.712 --> 29:00.578
 but I had a bit
 more confidence

29:00.680 --> 29:02.308
 than the first time around.

29:02.410 --> 29:03.949
 Enough to know
 that you can, in fact,

29:04.051 --> 29:07.619
 get away with murder
 and even get rewarded for it.

29:07.721 --> 29:09.621
BECKET: Super voyeuristic,
right?

29:09.723 --> 29:11.358
It's like the artist,
you know,

29:11.460 --> 29:12.819
is making us an accomplice.

29:12.921 --> 29:13.952
Making us complicit.

29:14.054 --> 29:16.692
It's just so genius.

29:16.794 --> 29:19.291
-The artist, huh?
-Yeah.

29:19.393 --> 29:21.461
(TECHNO MUSIC CONTINUES
OVER SPEAKERS)

29:25.003 --> 29:26.972
I'm sorry to ask this,
you're not...

29:27.906 --> 29:29.737
You're not Noah Redfellow,
are you?

29:29.839 --> 29:32.278
I don't know. Am I?

29:32.380 --> 29:35.340
Oh, I'm just...

29:35.442 --> 29:37.917
-I'm just a huge fan.
-Wow. (CHUCKLES)

29:38.684 --> 29:40.821
Yeah, I get a lot of that.

29:40.923 --> 29:43.182
I... I try not to come around
too much on Fridays

29:43.284 --> 29:44.889
'cause, you know,
people be on me.

29:44.992 --> 29:46.219
Yeah, no, no, no.
I don't want to bother you.

29:46.321 --> 29:48.725
-I'm sorry. Yeah.
-No, no, no. You know...

29:48.827 --> 29:49.989
You know what I mean? Like...

29:50.091 --> 29:51.331
You know, this next part

29:51.433 --> 29:52.965
may sound kinda crazy.

29:54.769 --> 29:57.035
I think you and I
actually may be cousins.

29:58.837 --> 30:00.771
-What?
-(LAUGHING) I know.

30:00.873 --> 30:02.541
So... so, you grew up
in Newark?

30:02.643 --> 30:04.003
-Newark, yeah.
-Nice, dude!

30:04.105 --> 30:06.179
That is grimy as fuck!

30:06.281 --> 30:07.709
I mean, sure.

30:07.811 --> 30:09.647
Yo, do you develop?

30:09.750 --> 30:11.849
-Uh, what? Do I...
-Do you...

30:11.951 --> 30:13.080
Do you develop,
like, your photos?

30:13.182 --> 30:16.489
Oh, um...
No, not... not really.

30:16.591 --> 30:18.183
No, man. You've gotta develop.

30:18.285 --> 30:20.387
It drives me fucking crazy!

30:20.489 --> 30:22.056
You know, no one develops,
and it's like, okay,

30:22.158 --> 30:23.356
-if you're totally broke.
-Yeah.

30:23.459 --> 30:25.595
Yeah, I get it.
Or if you don't have space.

30:25.697 --> 30:28.334
Oh, fuck! Okay, um,

30:28.436 --> 30:29.529
kick that beer.

30:29.631 --> 30:31.067
-Kick... kick the beer?
-(CAMERA SHUTTER CLICKING)

30:31.169 --> 30:32.937
NOAH: Yeah, kick the beer.
Go ahead. Yeah!

30:33.039 --> 30:34.501
BECKET: Like... Like that?
NOAH: Yeah, nice.

30:35.970 --> 30:37.140
Dude, that's us.

30:37.242 --> 30:39.644
We're all just fucking
drunk girls at a party.

30:39.746 --> 30:41.039
We don't know what's going on.

30:41.141 --> 30:43.510
(DOOR SLIDING OPEN)

30:45.683 --> 30:46.887
NOAH: Huh?

30:48.022 --> 30:50.891
This whole shit was made
by a company in Lithuania

30:50.993 --> 30:54.059
out of, uh, recycled
toothbrushes or tampons, or...

30:54.594 --> 30:56.395
It's, uh, medical.

30:56.497 --> 30:58.624
(LAUGHS) You smoke?

31:00.335 --> 31:01.327
-Uh, no.
-No?

31:01.429 --> 31:03.167
-(LIGHTER FLICKING)
-Ooh, I'm dyin'.

31:03.864 --> 31:05.169
Mm.

31:08.505 --> 31:10.636
Yo, you really want to know
what we're doing up here?

31:11.379 --> 31:12.107
(CHUCKLES) What?

31:12.209 --> 31:14.012
(LAUGHS) I got
the old lady thinking

31:14.114 --> 31:17.182
I quit this shit in April
but I obviously did not,

31:17.284 --> 31:20.280
and so now I'm just in a shack
like the fucking Unabomber

31:20.382 --> 31:22.886
just... (PUFFING) ...smoking,
you know?

31:22.988 --> 31:25.523
-Shit.
-It's all fun and games

31:25.625 --> 31:27.024
until they turn 30,

31:27.127 --> 31:30.029
and then they start
asking for shit, right?

31:31.228 --> 31:32.293
-Am I right?
-For... for sure, yeah.

31:32.395 --> 31:33.827
NOAH: For sure.
That's what I'm saying.

31:33.929 --> 31:34.996
WOMAN: Hello?

31:35.099 --> 31:36.266
Mm, fuck.
Speak of the devil.

31:37.338 --> 31:38.233
Um...

31:38.336 --> 31:40.704
You can keep a secret, right?
We're family?

31:40.806 --> 31:42.273
-Yeah.
